WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.437 --> 00:00:12.766
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.766 --> 00:00:15.750
PETER DER MANUELIAN: We have all of this archeological evidence

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:18.990
and documentation for Giza from the great excavations

00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:21.510
in the early part of the 1900s.

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:24.600
So why move into visualization and 3D?

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:26.370
Can that help us with our scholarship?

00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:28.500
Is there something new to learn here?

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:31.410
Well, take a look at some of these examples with me.

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:35.220
'Where's the best place to study Giza?' is an interesting question to ask.

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:37.450
Of course, out at the site is a wonderful place,

00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:39.450
and I'm never going to advocate that no one ever

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:43.890
go there or use some kind of substitute for visiting the site of Giza itself.

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:46.660
It is a spectacular experience.

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:49.390
However, in many ways, you can study Giza better

00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:51.330
from archival documentation.

00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:54.540
And I will argue even from 3D visualization.

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:57.720
So take a look at this photograph from about 1927.

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:00.360
And if you look at the condition of this scene today,

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:02.910
unfortunately, it's in much sadder shape.

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:04.769
The figures are deteriorated.

00:01:04.769 --> 00:01:07.000
It's hard to recognize what's going on.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:09.930
So there's a case where we turn to the archives.

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:10.680
Or how about this.

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:12.750
We're now in the Berlin museum, and you're

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:15.150
seeing the facade of the tomb of Merib.

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:17.880
If you went back to Giza, you would see a hole in the ground

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:21.150
because this chapel was removed in the 1840s.

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:24.690
So through 3D visualization, such as you see in this model,

00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:29.010
we can virtually repatriate the Chapel of Merib back to Giza

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:33.690
and experience it right where it originally belonged.

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:38.880
Another example of then and now you see a man carrying an oryx or a gazelle

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:40.710
on the left in pretty good condition.

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:44.250
And on the right, unfortunately, suffering terribly.

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:47.970
So our approach is to try to visualize the entire Giza Plateau.

00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:51.240
That's not to eliminate all the great archeological evidence, the dig

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:54.360
photos, and diaries, and maps or a visit to study

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:56.250
the originals up close and personal.

00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:57.900
All of these things are critical.

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:00.600
But by making a 3D model of the site, we can now

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:04.050
get to areas that normal humans don't have access to,

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:07.020
and we can start to ask some new research questions that

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:09.520
were previously impossible.

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:12.130
And the important point is this isn't a video game.

00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:15.960
It's based on the sound archeological evidence of many, many decades

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:18.580
of hard work by our predecessors.

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:21.300
So that means starting with maps and plans and section drawings

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:24.120
and building up computer models in wireframes,

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:29.820
and then texture mapping them and adding different types of stone casing types

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:31.320
and other types of colors.

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:36.600
And eventually, we get a photo-realistic appearance of these buildings.

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:41.010
It also means that we can repatriate or build 3D models of specific objects

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:44.730
and put them right back into the rooms that they were originally found in.

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:49.350
So this enhances our understanding of the archeological context of the site.

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:52.440
Something we don't get, of course, if we visit Gaza today.

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:55.740
Many of these objects are now in museums.

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:58.860
And since we're paying close attention to the archeological record,

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:01.650
we could take something like the tomb Hetepheres, which

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:04.440
was found in 1925 with so much of the wood

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:08.880
and materials deteriorated that it was just a pile of debris on the floor.

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:13.260
But thanks to the careful documentation, that has allowed us to build a 3D model

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:16.580
and even restore all of the Queens furniture.

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:19.290
You'll learn more about that in another module.

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:23.150
I'm often fond of saying, I would love to see the "tomb's-eye view" of Giza.

00:03:23.150 --> 00:03:26.430
In other words, to dive down into the underground limestone bedrock

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:29.310
and look upwards and see all of those wonderful burial

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:31.650
shafts leading up into the mastaba tombs.

00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:33.930
Well, with 3D modeling, we can do that.

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:36.090
Mortals can dive into limestone.

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:39.360
But we can hover around and rotate around burial shafts

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:42.540
and get a real sense of how these substructures of these buildings

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:46.080
connect and relate to the superstructures below ground

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:49.240
and above ground.

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:53.070
And in this particular image, you see the original excavation photo

00:03:53.070 --> 00:03:55.650
of a burial chamber in 1906.

00:03:55.650 --> 00:04:00.010
And then the modeling, the wireframe, and then the completed reconstruction.

00:04:00.010 --> 00:04:01.560
This is a room that is underground.

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:03.270
It's completely sanded over again.

00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:05.880
And no one has been there since 1906.

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:08.940
And of course, 1906 was black and white photography.

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:13.050
So through our 3D modeling, we can recreate aspects of this chamber

00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:16.050
faithfully reproduced based on the archeological evidence,

00:04:16.050 --> 00:04:21.750
but now in color and now with complete freedom of movement.

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:24.480
Eventually, we'll be building all of the Giza Plateau,

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:27.960
and we hope to lend the kind of detail and meticulous care

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:30.330
to the structure of every single mastaba.

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:33.270
Its casing types, its shapes of limestone blocks,

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:34.800
its chapel decoration.

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:37.320
This is a big job, and it may involve crowdsourcing.

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:42.780
But one by one, we can actually model the entire site in faithful detail.

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:45.240
In terms of the royal temples, we too have things

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:47.430
to learn here from visualization.

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:51.030
On the floor of Khafre's Valley Temple here, you see the emplacements

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:55.380
or is rectangular niches or holes in the floor partially restored today,

00:04:55.380 --> 00:05:00.210
but those originally contained the bases of statues of Khafre.

00:05:00.210 --> 00:05:01.880
The question is, how big were they?

00:05:01.880 --> 00:05:03.000
Were the platforms big?

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:04.350
Were the statues big?

00:05:04.350 --> 00:05:08.070
So by placing our 3D models in these emplacements in the floor,

00:05:08.070 --> 00:05:11.010
we can start to experiment, and get a sense of how big

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:14.490
or how small they should be reconstructed.

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:18.030
Here is a very colorful rendering of the Menkaure Valley Temple.

00:05:18.030 --> 00:05:20.550
Remember this was built in stone by Menkaure,

00:05:20.550 --> 00:05:24.780
but he probably died too soon, and so his successor and son Shepseskaf

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:26.970
tried to finish it in mud brick.

00:05:26.970 --> 00:05:29.580
It went through multiple building phases, indicated

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:33.900
by the white and the pinkish colors here, and then was actually inhabited.

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:36.240
All of those dwellings in the courtyard came later

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:37.920
towards the end of the Old Kingdom.

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:41.520
So we can almost magically pick these elements apart

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:44.480
and look at the temple at various points in time.

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:48.300
Empty, full, complete, half complete, limestone, mud brick,

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:50.310
all with different color coding.

00:05:50.310 --> 00:05:53.160
That's something you don't get when you visit Giza itself.

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:56.580
This temple is completely buried, you won't see a thing today.

00:05:56.580 --> 00:05:59.760
There are also ways to experiment with what you see on the walls.

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:03.600
In this example, we can actually point to items in the tomb

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:07.350
carvings and paintings such as furniture belonging to this queen.

00:06:07.350 --> 00:06:11.160
And if we click on an element, it pops off the wall as a 3D model,

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:15.382
which we can then rotate in space and get a better understanding of.

00:06:15.382 --> 00:06:17.340
Imagine if you could walk into a tomb and click

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:20.640
on absolutely anything that's represented on the chapel walls,

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:23.010
and have it pop into 3D real life for you.

00:06:25.570 --> 00:06:27.810
Here's an example of some famous tombs that

00:06:27.810 --> 00:06:29.910
have nothing surviving above ground.

00:06:29.910 --> 00:06:33.540
No superstructure, just the subterranean decorated chambers.

00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:36.390
So here again, with 3D modeling, we can experiment

00:06:36.390 --> 00:06:39.120
what should be over these tomb areas.

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:40.200
Was it a mastaba?

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:41.460
Was it an enclosure wall?

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:43.150
Was it something simpler?

00:06:43.150 --> 00:06:45.870
So once again, experimenting with visualization

00:06:45.870 --> 00:06:50.880
can help us understand the development of this part of the cemetery further.

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:54.420
Sometimes you have to draw a clear line between what you're restoring

00:06:54.420 --> 00:06:57.540
and what you know about what's archeologically attested.

00:06:57.540 --> 00:07:00.180
So in this case, we're looking from the air at that dark patch

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:02.040
on the east side of the Great Pyramid.

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:06.870
That is all that remains of the basalt flooring of Khufu's pyramid temple.

00:07:06.870 --> 00:07:08.430
Down below is a 3D model.

00:07:08.430 --> 00:07:12.840
In this case, rather hypothetical based on other mortuary temples

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:16.140
that are from the same period, and with the same basalt flooring.

00:07:16.140 --> 00:07:19.620
But again we have to delineate carefully what we know

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:24.330
and what we don't know what we are theoretically restoring.

00:07:24.330 --> 00:07:26.940
We can even put human characters back into the scene.

00:07:26.940 --> 00:07:31.170
This lends a sense of proportion, helps us understand the use of various areas,

00:07:31.170 --> 00:07:32.530
and buildings better.

00:07:32.530 --> 00:07:34.980
So in this view, you're looking at the central field.

00:07:34.980 --> 00:07:38.370
That's the area now choked with tombs behind the Sphinx,

00:07:38.370 --> 00:07:39.780
but before it was a cemetery.

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:43.350
It was the Quarry zone for all of the core limestone blocks

00:07:43.350 --> 00:07:45.000
that went into the Great Pyramid.

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:47.280
And here you see thousands and thousands of Egyptians

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:51.210
hard at work in the Quarry carving the individual blocks that would then

00:07:51.210 --> 00:07:55.410
be towed on sledges up to the Great Pyramid, which is just barely rising

00:07:55.410 --> 00:07:57.270
in the distance in this model.

00:07:57.270 --> 00:07:59.340
Simulations can help us as well.

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:02.370
Here's the subterranean chapel of Queen Meresankh.

00:08:02.370 --> 00:08:05.970
And the arrow points to a window leading into the chapel.

00:08:05.970 --> 00:08:10.320
Once we go inside, we can see if we reconstruct the course of the sunlight,

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:13.320
that the sun pierces through that window right through the room

00:08:13.320 --> 00:08:17.100
and illuminates the false door or the sacred offering place

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:18.420
in another chamber.

00:08:18.420 --> 00:08:21.690
That's something we wouldn't have known without building the 3D modeling.

00:08:21.690 --> 00:08:25.170
And in fact, we can now think about where other neighboring buildings

00:08:25.170 --> 00:08:28.710
and would they have blocked the sun coming into that window?

00:08:28.710 --> 00:08:32.700
If so, does that tell us which tombs were first and which tombs were second?

00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:35.580
And in fact, help us chronologically reconstruct

00:08:35.580 --> 00:08:40.860
this part of the cemetery all based on a few shafts of sunlight in a window.

00:08:40.860 --> 00:08:42.309
And now here's a case study.

00:08:42.309 --> 00:08:44.130
This is a chapel of a man named Nefer.

00:08:44.130 --> 00:08:46.470
He built this exterior stone chapel.

00:08:46.470 --> 00:08:49.470
And where all the men are standing was once a beautifully

00:08:49.470 --> 00:08:52.380
decorated four walls of this room.

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:54.960
Now, this gets tricky, follow along with me.

00:08:54.960 --> 00:08:58.260
This entrance niche or jam is in Boston.

00:08:58.260 --> 00:09:02.460
On the other side of the entrance, this piece is now in Paris.

00:09:02.460 --> 00:09:04.420
If we go inside and look at the South wall,

00:09:04.420 --> 00:09:08.430
the upper part of the standing couple is in Boston.

00:09:08.430 --> 00:09:12.100
The rest of their bodies are still at Giza in the tomb.

00:09:12.100 --> 00:09:16.560
We can put them together, and you can see them now reunited.

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:19.020
Then over to the west wall, it gets even more confusing.

00:09:19.020 --> 00:09:21.750
This little block with part of the name of Nefer's wife

00:09:21.750 --> 00:09:23.970
is in Birmingham England.

00:09:23.970 --> 00:09:27.360
Right next door are two connecting pieces in Copenhagen.

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:31.950
And they also join a fragment still on the chapel wall itself at Giza.

00:09:31.950 --> 00:09:34.320
Next to that is the upper part of the false door.

00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:36.540
This is in Rome.

00:09:36.540 --> 00:09:39.000
Put it all together, and it makes quite a jigsaw puzzle.

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:41.700
We're never going to do this physically, so here we're

00:09:41.700 --> 00:09:43.080
just doing it photographically.

00:09:43.080 --> 00:09:46.140
But the next step, of course, is 3D modeling.

00:09:46.140 --> 00:09:47.970
There's nothing on the south wall.

00:09:47.970 --> 00:09:51.270
But around onto the east wall, we have more pieces.

00:09:51.270 --> 00:09:54.300
In this case, another fragment in Copenhagen and that one

00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:58.440
joins to a standing representation of Nofer and his spouse

00:09:58.440 --> 00:10:00.570
still on the chapel wall.

00:10:00.570 --> 00:10:03.030
So putting all these pieces together virtually

00:10:03.030 --> 00:10:05.880
is really the only way to understand this tomb.

00:10:05.880 --> 00:10:08.670
From there, we can start to colorize the scenes,

00:10:08.670 --> 00:10:11.600
understand the graphic inscriptions better, and in fact,

00:10:11.600 --> 00:10:16.750
the relationships of all the decoration on these walls.

00:10:16.750 --> 00:10:21.200
We can even recreate a 3D model of Reisner's Harvard camp, the expedition

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:23.900
headquarters to the west of Khafre's pyramid.

00:10:23.900 --> 00:10:25.770
Here you see a rendering of the courtyard,

00:10:25.770 --> 00:10:30.510
which was always full of statues and ceramics and stone vessel fragments.

00:10:30.510 --> 00:10:35.000
This was the central headquarter hub where the expedition based everything,

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:39.530
wrote their publications, held court, entertained visitors, showed colleagues

00:10:39.530 --> 00:10:40.340
around.

00:10:40.340 --> 00:10:43.880
And this was really an integral part of the entire story

00:10:43.880 --> 00:10:47.620
of the excavation of the Giza necropolis.

